  "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace since in does not contain the zero-vector.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\".",
